  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-03): Moore output decoded from a state `case` was lowered into
    `always_ff` (REGISTERED, `out <= …`) instead of `always_comb`, lagging the
    simulator one cycle (verilator FAIL / trace PASS).
      TRUE ROOT CAUSE (my first "single-register" hypothesis was wrong): the trigger
      is a NON-EXHAUSTIVE state `case` — a register whose encoding leaves unreachable
      values (6 states in a 3-bit reg; a `u8` pc with 2 states). ports_driven_all_paths
      refused to count ANY output as fully driven on a case with no default that isn't
      exhaustive, so an output written on every explicit arm still looked "conditional"
      and got moved to always_ff. traffic_light is immune only because 4 states = 2^2
      is exactly exhaustive. (Nothing to do with register count.)
      FIX: copper-codegen/src/vlir_lower.rs `hoist_moore_output_defaults` — for an
      output that is currently conditional AND driven on every explicit arm, hoist a
      top-of-`always_comb` default (`out = <first-arm value>;`) and give the case an
      empty `default` (Verilator CASEINCOMPLETE is an error). Narrow by design:
      exhaustive cases (traffic_light) are byte-identical; a genuine enabled-register
      hold driven in only some arms (bsg_dff_en) is untouched and stays `always_ff`
      (both verified). seq6 → full equivalence (tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs).
      RESIDUAL (separate, still open): see the Mealy-output item directly below.
  ✔ RESOLVED-BY-RESTRICTION (entry was STALE, corrected 2026-08-24): the MEALY
    output sim-vs-SV divergence flagged 2026-08-03 is no longer open, but note it
    was NOT fixed — it was ruled out of the language. The multi-write-around-a-tick
    guardrail rejects a plain (combinational) `Out` in that shape and points at
    `RegOut`, which reconciles with the transpiled `always_ff`. So the divergent
    program can no longer be written, rather than the divergence being repaired.
      EVIDENCE the entry was stale: tests/nested_tick_equivalence.rs and
      tests/fsm_six_state_equivalence.rs both run FULL sim ≡ transpiled-SV
      equivalence now; the only `sim_only` occurrences left in either file are
      historical comments. This entry still said "Meanwhile ... runs sim_only".
      PAPER NOTE: cite this as a scope restriction, not a repair — it belongs with
      contribution 5 (the `Out`/`RegOut` pair), not with the Moore-output fix.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — RegOut written UNCONDITIONALLY in a
    multi-phase loop was emitted as a continuous `assign`, not a flip-flop.
      ROOT CAUSE: vlir_lower::lower_flat_stmts turned EVERY top-level
      SHIRStmt::PortDrive into a module-level VLIRContinuousAssign. Those assigns
      are collected separately from the phase's statements and never passed
      through split_output_regs — so the port's `registered` flag was silently
      ignored for unconditional writes, and the phase guard was lost with it. The
      output then followed a phase-gated combinational value and collapsed to 0
      on the cycles where that phase was inactive.
      WHY mac_fsm WAS ALWAYS FINE: it writes its RegOut INSIDE a conditional,
      which lowers to a PortAssign in the phase statements — which
      split_output_regs does see. Only the unconditional shape was broken.
      FIX: one arm in lower_flat_stmts — a PortDrive targeting a registered
      output becomes a PortAssign instead of a continuous assign, so the existing
      split moves it into always_ff under the phase guard. lower_flat_stmts now
      takes `registered_outs`; the combinational caller passes an empty set (a
      comb module has no clock, so no registered outputs).
      THE SIM WAS THE CORRECT SIDE: it matches BaseJump's own sipo_block.sv,
      which registers data_o in an always_ff.
      REGRESSION COVER: tests/regout_multiphase_equivalence.rs — a behavioural
      sim ≡ SV check PLUS a shape assertion (`word_o <=` present, `assign word_o`
      absent), because the behavioural check alone could pass by luck on the
      sampled cycles.
      NEW COVERAGE UNLOCKED: tests/sipo_block_equivalence.rs — sipo_block now has
      sim ≡ transpiled SV on top of the example's existing sim ≡ BaseJump check,
      so the generated SV is transitively anchored to third-party hardware. It
      `include!`s the example (with #[cfg(not(test))] on its main) rather than
      copying it, so there is one source of truth. All five outputs compared once
      the plain-Out fix below landed.

  ✔ FIXED (2026-08-24, with user sign-off) — a PLAIN `Out` written in only SOME
    PHASES of a multi-phase loop was emitted as an unguarded continuous assign, so
    it dropped to 0 outside its phase instead of holding.
      SAME MECHANISM as the RegOut bug above (top-level PortDrive → unguarded
      continuous assign over a phase-gated wire defaulting to '0), different port
      kind. Sim gave 1,1,3,3; the SV gave 0,2,0,4.
      THE SIM WAS THE CORRECT SIDE: a sequential plain `Out` HOLDS when unwritten
      (the enabled-register idiom, verified against BaseJump's bsg_dff_en), so a
      write in one phase is EFFECTIVELY conditional and must become an
      implicit-hold register — the same treatment a write on some-but-not-all
      PATHS already got.
      WHY THE RegOut PATCH ALONE WAS NOT ENOUGH: conditional_output_ports decides
      conditionality by finding writes nested in a conditional. A phase-top-level
      write is unconditional WITHIN its phase, so routing it to a PortAssign left
      it unrecognised. lower_seq therefore also computes
      `phase_scoped_output_ports` from the SHIR. It has to be done at SHIR level:
      the VLIR-level ports_driven_any_path cannot see an unconditional drive,
      because that has already been moved out of the statement list. The two sets
      are unioned into one `hold_outs`, so RegOut and phase-scoped plain Out share
      one mechanism.
      ⚠️ THE RULE KEYS ON THE DRIVEN VALUE, NOT ON PHASE COVERAGE — AND THE FIRST
      ATTEMPT GOT THIS WRONG. "Written in some phases but not all" LAGGED
      mac_pipeline's output by one cycle (expected 10 got 0, expected 37 got 10);
      caught by the full regression, tests/mac_pipeline_equivalence.rs is the
      measured witness. A top-level drive lowers to an UNGUARDED continuous
      assign, so it reads its RHS every cycle; correctness depends on what the RHS
      is OUTSIDE the writing phase:
        - mac_pipeline: `out.write(sum)` reads the inferred REGISTER sum_r, which
          RETAINS its value → `assign out = sum_r` is already correct, and
          registering it again just adds a cycle.
        - sipo_block / the fixture: the drive reads a PHASE-LOCAL COMBINATIONAL
          WIRE that always_comb defaults to '0 → the assign propagates zeros.
      Only the second needs the hold. Helpers: shir_expr_vars,
      shir_phase_local_wires, shir_port_drives.
      DELIBERATELY NARROW ON TWO COUNTS: a port written in EVERY phase is excluded
      (driven every cycle), and so is a port whose value is register-backed.
      Widening either lags an output by a cycle — do not widen without a measured
      witness.
      REGRESSION COVER: tests/plain_out_multiphase_equivalence.rs — behavioural
      sim ≡ SV plus a shape assertion (`seen_o <=` present, `assign seen_o`
      absent).
      COVERAGE UNLOCKED: tests/sipo_block_equivalence.rs now compares ALL FIVE
      outputs (data_o + the four wN_dbg), not just data_o. The wN_dbg ports expose
      the module's per-cycle internal capture, which is exactly what the
      mid-phase-read question sipo_block was built to answer.

  ✔ CONTROL EXTRACTION INCREMENT B — repeating waits  [DONE 2026-08-24]
     `loop { <test>; clk.tick().await; }` nested inside the module's own loop, left
     by `break`. Increment A flattened ticks nested in `if`/`match` by inlining each
     continuation into the branches; a nested loop adds the one thing that model
     lacked — a state that loops back to ITSELF.
     THE LOWERING: the loop gets a head state H; its body is lowered once with H as
     the back-edge target, so the body's trailing tick emits `pc = H` (stay another
     cycle) and `break` INLINES the loop's continuation (leave, in the same cycle —
     breaking is not a clock boundary). Entering must not cost a cycle either, so
     the already-lowered body is CLONED into the entry point. Cloning the lowered
     form rather than re-lowering the source is what keeps it cheap: the two copies
     share the sub-states their ticks allocated, so a wait costs ONE extra state,
     not a doubling. (The state-count worry recorded when this was proposed turned
     out not to bite, for that reason.)
     `if ready { break; }` also forces a split increment A would not have made: the
     branch holds no tick, but control still leaves it, so the continuation must be
     inlined per-branch anyway. "Contains a tick" became "diverges" as the splitting
     rule.
     → tests/wait_loop_equivalence.rs (4 tests): a single wait, TWO SEQUENTIAL
       waits with a tick between them, a shape pin that one wait costs one extra
       state, and the refused ordering. Stimulus uses uneven runs (2, 1, 3 cycles)
       so a wait that held for a FIXED number of cycles could not pass.
     BOTH NEGATIVE CONTROLS MEASURED: making the back edge target state 0 instead of
     the loop head broke the two-wait handshake (a single wait cannot tell the two
     apart — which is why the handshake test exists); making `break` jump to a new
     state instead of inlining broke all three.

  ── THE MID-PHASE READ SEAM, MEASURED 2026-08-24 ────────────────────────────
  Went looking for one thing and found three. The refusal of
  `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` was blamed on a read-timing seam; that blame
  was PARTLY WRONG, and taking it apart turned up a silent miscompilation that had
  nothing to do with control flow.

  ✔ FIXED — A REGISTER REWRITTEN INSIDE ONE BRANCH LOST EVERY WRITE BUT THE FIRST.
     `shir_lower::extract_updates_from_stmts` merges a branch's register updates
     into one muxed next-value, and looked each register's value up with `.find()`
     — the FIRST update in the branch. A register written twice kept the earlier
     write:
         t <= (t + 4'd1);          // emitted
         //   `if t == 3 { t = 0; }` — GONE
     That is the mod-N counter idiom, i.e. how anyone writes a clock divider, and
     it silently produced a free-running counter that wrapped at its full width.
     Silent because the surviving value is perfectly well-typed.
     WHY IT SURVIVED THIS LONG: outside a branch the merge does not happen — the
     updates emit as separate non-blocking assignments and SV's last-write-wins
     makes the result right anyway. So it needed a register rewritten INSIDE an
     `if` or a `match` arm, and nothing in the corpus had one: every FSM arm here
     assigns each register once.
     FIX: `last_update_for` — take the branch's LAST assignment. One line, plus a
     name and a comment so the next reader knows why order matters.
     → tests/rewritten_register_equivalence.rs (3 tests: a `match`-arm divider, a
       rewrite inside a nested `if`, and a shape pin). All three fail with the fix
       reverted; the `if` case needed the two writes in the SAME branch, since a
       top-level write plus a branch write does not reproduce it.
     RECORD THE CLASS, not just the bug: a merge that silently keeps ONE of several
     candidates keeps the wrong one for as long as nothing produces two.

  ✔ THE SEAM IS NOT ABOUT CONTROL FLOW, and it is narrower than it looked.
     With the miscompilation fixed, `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` is CORRECT
     when the test reads a REGISTER — measured, sim ≡ SV over a divide-by-three
     wait. So the flattening (and the body rotation it needs — the head state is
     the one the tick ENTERS) was right all along.
     What still diverges is exactly one thing: an `In` read in the post-tick window.

  ✔ THE REMAINING SEAM — CLOSED AS REFUSED BY DESIGN  [2026-08-24, user's call]
     A post-tick `In` read is sampled by the SIMULATOR at the START of the window
     (`Immediate`, per SYNCHRONOUS_SEMANTICS.md: "consumes the value the just-past
     edge produced") and by the EMITTED HARDWARE at the END of it (a flip-flop
     samples just before its own edge). Under the harness convention "drive, then
     clock", those are different input values, one cycle apart.
     NOT a "which of two adjacent samples" question: holding each stimulus value
     for TWO cycles does not reconcile them, because the two models read in
     different WINDOWS, not at different points of the same one.
     DISPOSITION: the ordering `loop { clk.tick().await; <test> }` is OUTSIDE THE
     LANGUAGE. Not "unimplemented" — decided. The supported ordering
     (`loop { <test>; clk.tick().await; }`) expresses the same designs and is what
     one would write anyway, so the restriction costs nothing that works.
     THIS IS THE SAME DISPOSITION AS D1 (pre-tick alignment): the divergent program
     is made unwritable rather than the divergence adjudicated. Copper does not
     choose between the two samplings; it declines to let a design depend on which.
     PAPER NOTE: cite as a SCOPE RESTRICTION, not a repair — alongside D1 and the
     Mealy-output item, which are the same move. The honest framing of all three is
     "Copper infers the register/combinational boundary that other HDLs make
     explicit, and where inference cannot decide, the language narrows."
     WHY THE COST IS LOW, measured: the divergence needs an input that CHANGES
     MID-CYCLE. An `In` driven by a clocked module in the same domain is stable
     across the window, so both models read the same value. It is a
     testbench-observable difference — which is exactly why it could not be left
     in, since sim ≡ SV under a testbench is this project's bar.
     REJECTED ALTERNATIVES, recorded so they are not re-tried:
       - register the input at the entering edge so the hardware reproduces the
         simulator. Exact under any stimulus, and it would have allowed the
         ordering — but it silently inserts a flip-flop the user did not write and
         delays the input a cycle relative to the plain reading of the source.
       - declare the simulator normative only for edge-aligned inputs and require
         harnesses to hold inputs across the boundary. Changes no hardware and no
         language surface, but weakens the central check: sim ≡ SV would no longer
         hold under the testbench convention every existing test uses.
     WHAT IT DOES *NOT* UNBLOCK — measured 2026-08-24, correcting an earlier
     overstatement in this file ("settling it is what would put the CPU's control
     flow within reach"). The seam was the FOURTH thing standing between
     examples/cpu/rv32i_cpu.rs and transpilation, not the first:
       1. `program: In<Vec<Bits<32>>, MainClk>` — a `Vec` input port. A
          variable-length vector is not a wire bundle; this is not a missing
          feature so much as a value that is not a signal.
       2. `let flat = { let mut v = program.read(); v.resize(1024, …); v };` —
          host-side Rust running at construction.
       3. `from_contents(clk, flat.clone())` for imem AND dmem — a run-time
          computed preload, refused because there is nothing to emit.
       4. the 17 tick-then-test waits — the seam.
     Items 1-3 are one thing: the program image arrives as a host-side Rust value,
     which is how the example is WRITTEN (to be simulated with a program handed in
     by the testbench), not a transpiler gap. A synthesizable CPU takes its program
     from a fixed image (`from_fn`, or `from_contents` with literals — both work
     now) or fetches it over a bus port.
     ALSO WORTH KNOWING before assuming the CPU's waits could just be flipped to
     the supported ordering: of the 17, EIGHT wait on a *write* port's
     `is_ready()`, which the simulator always returns true for — they are "tick
     exactly once" written in a general form, and
     `loop { if wp.is_ready() { break; } tick; }` would never tick at all, which
     the reachability guarantee rejects. Only the nine read-port waits (READ_LAT=2)
     are genuine variable waits. So a transpilable CPU is a REWRITE, not a flip.
     Pinned by unsupported_constructs.rs::ticking_before_the_test_in_a_wait_is_unsupported.

  ☐ `RegOut` IS NOT IMMUNE TO SEQUENTIAL FORWARDING, contrary to
     PRETICK_ALIGNMENT_GUARDRAIL.md, which lists it as the first remedy and calls
     it "immune: it commits at the edge, so *when* the write executes is
     unobservable". Measured: with a `RegOut` written AFTER a register update in
     the same segment, codegen emits `out <= n` — the register's OLD value — while
     the simulator forwards the update and writes the new one. The D1 compile error
     exempts `RegOut`, so this shape passes the guardrail and diverges anyway.
     The immunity claim holds for the PHASE question it was written about (when the
     segment runs); it does not extend to forwarding WITHIN the segment. Worth
     correcting in the doc either way, and deciding whether D1's exemption should
     be narrowed.

  ☐ `continue` inside a nested loop. Jumping to the loop head mid-cycle needs the
     head's LOWERED body at a point where it is still being lowered. `break` avoids
     this because its target (the loop's continuation) is plain source. Refused with
     a spanned diagnostic; pinned.

  ── LATENT HOLES SURFACED BY THE MEMORY WORK, 2026-08-24 ────────────────────
  Each of these was invisible until a memory design happened to walk into it.
  None was caught by a failing example; two were caught by a corpus guard and one
  by Verilator's linter. Recorded here because the CLASS matters — a check that
  cannot see a construct reports green on it.

  ✔ FIXED — copper-analysis counted a MEMORY ARRAY as a flip-flop.
     `reference_sv_registers` strips bracket groups before scanning for `<=`
     targets, so a memory write `mem[a] <= d` reduced to `mem <= d` and was
     reported as a register named `mem`. No source-level inference can ever produce
     that name (`infer_registers` names locals live across a tick; a `Memory<..>`
     binding is not one), so `register_reconciliation` failed the moment a fixture
     contained a memory write.
     THE HOLE WAS ALREADY THERE, unrelated to codegen: the same miscount applies to
     any HAND-WRITTEN reference SV containing a RAM, which is the G2 path —
     examples/memory/sv/dual_port_ram.sv declares `reg [15:0] ram [1023:0]` and
     writes `ram[addra] <= dia`. Anyone calling `with_reference_registers` on a
     memory design would have compared against a register set with a phantom entry.
     FIX: unpacked-array declarations are excluded in `reference_sv_registers`, with
     unit tests BOTH ways — a memory is not a register, and a bit-assigned register
     (`q[3] <= …`) still is one.
     Related, same file, same shape of blind spot: the memory read PIPELINE
     registers (`<mem>_rd<N>_q` / `_v`) are synthesized by lowering and have no
     source-level name, so register_reconciliation now treats them like the
     phase/pc counter it already excluded.

  ☐ Multi-tick loops SILENTLY DROP combinational statements after the LAST tick.
     `shir_lower::lower_seq_body`'s multi-tick path never passes the trailing
     segment through `lower_pre_edge_stmts` — only the single-tick path does (where
     the trailing segment shares the one phase). Its REGISTER updates do flow, so
     the loss is partial and quiet: an `out.write(x)` after the last tick simply
     vanished, leaving an undriven port. Measured on a two-tick module with two
     outputs: the second had no driver at all in the emitted SV.
     NOW LOUD, not fixed — it is a clean error, pinned by
     unsupported_constructs.rs::trailing_combinational_statements_in_a_multi_tick_loop_are_rejected.
     DECIDING where those statements belong is a real semantics question (the
     trailing segment runs in the NEXT iteration's phase-0 window, but the existing
     model attributes its register updates to the LAST phase's edge), so it needs
     sign-off rather than a default. Note the two halves currently disagree, which
     is worth resolving at the same time.

  ☐ Copper does not reject an UNDRIVEN `Out` — only Verilator does.
     examples/memory/dual_port_ram.rs shipped a `data_out_a` port it never wrote.
     Harmless in simulation (the wire just keeps its initial value) and invisible
     until the module transpiled, where it is a fatal UNDRIVEN warning under -Wall.
     The port was removed, but the language gap stands: an output a module never
     drives is a design error the front end could catch with the analysis it
     already has (`ports_driven_all_paths` and friends live in vlir_lower).
     Care needed on the sequential side: a plain `Out` HOLDS when unwritten (the
     enabled-register idiom, verified against BaseJump's bsg_dff_en), so the rule
     is "never written on ANY path", not "not written on all paths".


