     [DEFERRED — needs a "what stays legal" definition; design-fuzzy, see MACRO]
  ☐ Warn on persistent-state mutation before the first tick
     [DEFERRED — it's a WARNING; trybuild asserts on errors, so needs a different
     test path (or promotion to an error). Design call pending.]
  ☐ Require direct tick await (reject aliased/wrapped `clk` tick)  [DEFERRED]
  ✔ LANDED (2026-08-03): reject a combinational `Out` written on both sides of ONE
     bare tick after a leading input read (`… inp.read() …; out.write(x); tick;
     out.write(y)`). The coroutine executor collapses such multi-write-around-a-tick
     outputs to the last write (silent sim ≠ SV). Rejected at the macro, pointing at
     `RegOut` (registered) or explicit per-state writes — the MyHDL "restrict the
     synthesizable subset" discipline; keeps every accepted program sim ≡ SV.
     - Detection = `copper_analysis::multi_write_collapse` (copper-analysis/src/cfg.rs).
       The naive "written in >1 phase" rule OVER-FLAGGED uart/rv32i/sipo_block; the
       landed rule needs all THREE conditions (bare tick, both-sides write, leading In
       read comb-reaching the pre-tick write) — empirically pinned + corpus-clean (flags
       ONLY the two control-extraction probes, now converted to RegOut).
     - Wired into copper-macros (sequential arm); trybuild ui/fail/multi_write_collapse.rs
       + ui/pass/multi_write_regout_ok.rs; 8 analysis unit tests. Fixtures if_tick/
       match_tick → RegOut (nested_tick upgraded sim_only → full Verilator equivalence).
     - Now recurses into nested ticking loops (a straddle inside a `for`/`while` that
       ticks is caught in its sub-CFG). Whole corpus clean (0 flags). Nested serializer
       stays clean (writes once per iteration).
     - DYNAMIC backstop CONSIDERED then DROPPED: a runtime "Out written in both settle
       passes" check FALSE-FIRED on legitimate combinational modules (a comb output
       re-settles in both phases — confirmed on copper-sim's own wired_passthrough test).
       A precise dynamic version would need per-task tick-resolution tracking; instead we
       extended the STATIC rule to nested loops (above), keeping everything compile-time,
       all-paths, and false-positive-free. Decision: static checks over dynamic ones.

