--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
P3 — CDC / multi-clock (TODO flags "how to handle multiple clocks" as unsettled)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ◐ Existing: cdc_crossing.rs, two_domain_hierarchy_cdc.rs
  ✔ sync_2ff (stdlib src/sync.rs) → directed latency test  [DONE 2026-08-21]
     → tests/cdc_synchronizer_anchor.rs. First cargo-test coverage of the library
     primitive at all (it was example-only; two_domain_hierarchy_cdc.rs used a
     private copy of the body). Four arms on one primitive: library generic in sim,
     concrete fixture (tests/fixtures/sync_2ff_dut.rs) asserted identical in sim,
     that fixture transpiled under Verilator, and an INDEPENDENT hand-written
     reference examples/cdc/sv/sync_2ff_ref.sv under Verilator.
     NOTE the ☐ above said "delays by 2 receiver ticks" — MEASURED, that is wrong:
     the primitive's observable latency is ONE destination cycle (d standing at
     edge n appears on q after edge n+1), while holding two flip-flops. Both the
     independent Verilog and the transpiled SV agree. The "2 cycles" in
     examples/cdc/two_domain_counter.rs's prose counts the full path from a
     fast-domain register's output, including the producer's own registered delay.
     Also covered: rate-independence (1:1/2:1/3:1/5:1/8:1 give identical
     destination-cycle traces), 2-flop-vs-1-flop behavioral distinction, and
     pulse-at-edge captured vs pulse-between-edges dropped.
  ✔ Independent two-clock design: other-domain tasks quiescent under one tick
     → cdc_synchronizer_anchor.rs::source_domain_ticks_do_not_advance_the_synchronizer
     (20 source ticks with d toggling every one; q must not move).
  ✔ G1 TIMING_COVERAGE_MATRIX pattern 5 (CDC/synchronizer latency) — CLOSED.
     All 6 timing patterns are now anchored to independent hardware.
  ✔ BUG FIXED (found + fixed 2026-08-21, with user sign-off): infer_registers
     under-reported the 2-FF synchronizer — ONE flip-flop where the sim's behavior,
     the independent Verilog, and codegen all have TWO.
     ROOT CAUSE: Cfg::registers keyed only on "live across a TICK edge". `ff2` is
     defined post-tick (`ff2 = ff1`) and read pre-tick (`q.write(ff2)`), so walking
     backwards from the use, its own definition kills it before the tick is reached —
     its live range crosses the loop BACK EDGE, not a tick. It is nonetheless a real
     flop: `ff2 = ff1` reads ff1's PRE-edge value (the next line overwrites it), which
     a wire cannot reproduce — `assign ff2 = ff1` tracks the post-edge value and
     collapses the two stages into one flop (the exact failure src/sync.rs's own
     comment warns about).
     FIX: one line — Cfg::registers now unions live_out[head] (the loop head is an
     empty node, so its live-out IS the set carried across the back edge) with the
     across-tick set. General principle documented on the fn: a local defined in a
     post-tick segment had its defining expression evaluated against pre-edge values,
     so if it survives to the next iteration it needs storage. A post-tick temp that
     dies in its own segment is still excluded (D-input logic).
     VALIDATED against a differential oracle, not by argument: inference vs codegen's
     emitted flip-flops over every clocked module that transpiles in tests/fixtures +
     examples + src — 41/41 AGREE (was 38/41), synchronizers included, nothing newly
     over-reported. (register_reconciliation.rs itself scans only tests/fixtures: 24
     clocked modules, all green.)
     Both production call sites of infer_registers only log::debug! it, so no emitted
     hardware changed.
     REGRESSION COVER: register_reconciliation.rs no longer filters out
     #[hardware(synchronizer)] (is_sequential → is_clocked), so the shape is pinned
     corpus-wide; two new copper-analysis unit tests (post_tick_def_read_pre_tick_is_
     a_register + post_tick_temp_dying_in_its_own_segment_is_not_a_register); and
     cdc_synchronizer_anchor.rs::register_inference_matches_the_independent_reference
     is now a real NameExact G2 match instead of a pinned gap.
  ✔ D1 (pre-tick alignment) GUARDED 2026-08-21 — plan of record:
     design_docs/PRETICK_ALIGNMENT_GUARDRAIL.md (phases 0-3 done, gates G0-G3 met).
     RULE: a plain combinational `Out` driven FROM A REGISTER in the pre-tick segment,
     when that segment also assigns a register with NO `In` read preceding it.
     `RegOut` is immune by construction (commits at the edge) — that was the
     discriminator, found by changing ONLY the port type on otherwise-identical
     modules. Keying on the OUTPUT WRITE rather than the register is what made the
     rule shippable; an earlier register-keyed rule rejected mac_fsm (the G2
     name-exact reference).
     ADJUDICATED against independent hardware; every clause has a measured witness.
     Opt-out `#[hardware(sequential, allow_pretick_alignment)]` for demonstration
     fixtures only — silences the error, NOT the detection (the corpus test still
     counts them, so an opted-out module cannot vanish quietly).
     THREE candidate fixes tried and REJECTED with evidence, all recorded so they are
     not re-tried: uniform pre-edge barrier (22/654 corpus damage), register-keyed
     static rule (false-positived mac_fsm), Prost-style codegen lowering (fixes D1
     exactly, breaks the barrier-protected majority exactly — and that symmetry is
     the general result: no single lowering can match a simulator whose alignment is
     incidental).
     MIGRATED: fast_counter x3 → post-tick sticky update; slow_consumer x3 →
     trailing read. two_domain_hierarchy_cdc.rs now passes FOR THE RIGHT REASON
     (it previously passed because D1 and D2 cancelled).
  ✔ D2 FIXED 2026-08-21 (in the simulator, not by restriction). A read feeding a
     combinational `Out` in a segment that assigns NO register is now `Immediate`,
     not `Deferred` — `copper_analysis::classify_reads`. The barrier does two jobs,
     deferring the read AND pinning the segment's phase; only the second is ever
     needed, and pinning a register-free segment makes a wire behave like a flop.
     ADJUDICATED against independent hand-written Verilog first (a clocked producer
     feeding a passthrough gives mid == out; sim gave mid/out = 1/0 2/1 3/2 before,
     1/1 2/2 3/3 after).
     FIRST ATTEMPT REJECTED: keying on the MODULE ("no registers anywhere") broke 3
     currently-passing tests including det_010_awaits_matches_independent_verilog — a
     HARDWARE-ANCHORED test. Cause: det_010_awaits and if_tick have no data registers
     but read inside control flow whose TICK COUNT depends on the sampled value, so
     their phase genuinely matters. The narrowed per-read rule excludes condition
     positions and fixes that.
     CORPUS: 666/667, the single failure being the test that pinned the old behavior.
     The CDC designs' trailing-read workaround was REVERTED — the natural
     `loop { out.write(in.read()); tick; }` idiom is correct again.
  ✔ Q5 ANSWERED 2026-08-21 — AND THE PREMISE WAS WRONG. The guardrail examines only
     the pre-tick segment, recorded as a known false negative citing the multi-tick
     `accum_2`. **accum_2 does not diverge.** Its test was #[ignore]d as "sim and
     transpiler disagree by one cycle ... adjudication pending" but it PASSES — and
     passes WITHOUT the D2 fix (checked by reverting cfg.rs alone), so earlier
     read-timing work had repaired it and the ignore went stale. Nobody noticed
     because an ignored test prints nothing. UN-IGNORED; stable over 3 runs.
     So the rule has NO known false negative — the middle-segment gap is theoretical.
     Measure an instance before widening it.
     ALSO: probe_registered_timing and probe_mem_latency are eprintln! DIAGNOSTICS
     with no assertions — passing means nothing. Their ignore reasons now say so
     instead of "temp investigation", which read like a deferred check.
     REMAINING IGNORED: probe_fsm_sim_matches_verilog only — correctly, it is the D1
     demonstration and still diverges by design.
  ✔ Q5 ANSWERED 2026-08-21 — AND THE PREMISE WAS WRONG. The guardrail examines only
     the pre-tick segment, recorded as a known false negative citing multi-tick
     `accum_2`. **accum_2 does not diverge.** Its test was #[ignore]d as "sim and
     transpiler disagree by one cycle ... adjudication pending" but it PASSES — and
     passes WITHOUT the D2 fix (checked by reverting cfg.rs alone), so earlier
     read-timing work had repaired it and the ignore went stale. Nobody noticed
     because an ignored test prints nothing. UN-IGNORED; stable over 3 runs.
     So the rule has NO known false negative — the middle-segment gap is theoretical.
     Measure an instance before widening it.
     ALSO: probe_registered_timing and probe_mem_latency are eprintln! DIAGNOSTICS
     with no assertions — passing means nothing. Their ignore reasons now say so
     instead of "temp investigation", which read like a deferred check.
     REMAINING IGNORED: probe_fsm_sim_matches_verilog only — correctly, it is the D1
     demonstration and still diverges by design.
  ✔ two_domain_counter → behavioral test  [DONE 2026-08-21]
     → tests/two_domain_counter_behavioral.rs (5 tests). The example self-checked only
     the BOUNDARY (flag high from slow cycle 5, one ratio, last signal in the chain) —
     which passes equally well when stages are individually wrong and cancel, exactly
     what happened to two_domain_hierarchy_cdc.rs. This asserts the PER-STAGE
     decomposition at 1:1/2:1/3:1: synchronizer costs exactly one slow cycle
     (rate-independent), the passthrough consumer adds zero, the crossing is monotone,
     the flag never leads the threshold it latches, counter advances at the source rate.
     ALSO FIXED THE EXAMPLE: its "flag_raw" column displayed a PROXY (fast_count >= 8),
     not the wire — showing the flag at slow 3 when the real wire asserts at 4. Now
     reads the real intermediate wires (In is Clone), adds a sync_q column, drops the
     dead raw_display/flag_raw vars (unused-variable warning), and the prose no longer
     claims "2-slow-cycle synchronizer latency" — measured, it is 1 + 0.
  ✔ flag_crossing → behavioral test (handoff semantics)  [DONE 2026-08-21]
     → tests/flag_crossing_behavioral.rs (5 tests). The example asserted only
     SELF-CONSISTENCY (recorded the sim's own output, compared it to itself, with a
     comment saying outright there is no Verilog reference). Now asserted at three
     ratios: a held flag crosses with exactly the synchronizer's latency (source and
     sink are passthroughs, adding none), a crossed flag stays high, a pulse standing
     at the edge crosses keeping its width, nothing crosses without a trigger.
     PINNED AS A FEATURE: a pulse that falls before the destination edge is DROPPED.
     That is the defining limitation of a bare 2-FF synchronizer and why a LEVEL
     crossing is safe while an EVENT crossing needs a toggle or handshake. Recorded so
     a later change cannot quietly "fix" it into something hardware cannot do.
  ✔ Multi-driver ACROSS domains — DEFINED: it is UNREPRESENTABLE  [DONE 2026-08-21]
     No behavior to define — the program cannot be written, and it fails on TWO
     independent counts, either fatal alone:
       1. a wire has exactly one write handle (`Out` and `RegOut` are !Clone), so a
          second driver is E0382 use-of-moved-value;
       2. domains are nominal, so a driver from another domain is E0308.
     Made EXECUTABLE rather than asserted: three new compile_fail doctests in
     copper-core/src/cdc.rs (the file whose doctests ARE the spec) — the moved-wire
     count, the wrong-domain count, and RegOut's single-driver guarantee, which had no
     doctest where `Out` already did. copper-core compile_fail doctests: 5 -> 8.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
P4 — Memory (first-class construct; MEMORY_DESIGN.md)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  ◐ Existing: memory_new.rs, mem_latency_probe.rs, verilog_fifo_memory_new.rs
  ✔ WRITE_LAT > 0 — ALREADY COVERED (this list was stale). copper-core/src/memory.rs
     has write_lat1_commits_after_one_tick + write_lat2_commits_after_two_ticks.
     Verified by reading the bodies, not the names: the LAT=2 test checks the write is
     INVISIBLE at posedge 1 and visible at posedge 2.
  ✔ Read-during-write, both modes — ALREADY COVERED (stale too):
     read_first_sees_old_value_on_same_addr_write,
     write_first_sees_new_value_on_same_addr_write, plus a different-address control.
  ✔ Multi-port arbitration at scale  [DONE 2026-08-22]
     → tests/memory_multiport_arbitration.rs (7 tests, 4R/4W). The existing two-port
     test CANNOT pin the rule: "highest index wins", "second one wins" and "last
     issued wins" all agree at two ports and disagree at four. One test therefore
     issues the contest in DESCENDING source order — source-order would give 0xB0, and
     the answer is 0xB3, so the rule is port INDEX priority. Also: partial contests,
     uncontested parallel writes, 4 independent reads, all read ports agreeing on the
     winner, and arbitration holding under WRITE_LAT=2.
  ✔ Out-of-range address — DEFINED: DELIBERATE PANIC  [DONE 2026-08-22, user's call]
     Was already a panic, but an INCIDENTAL one: raw "index out of bounds: the len is
     4 but the index is 104" from inside advance_write_pipelines, at the CLOCK EDGE,
     naming neither the memory nor the port. Now `copper-core::memory::out_of_range`
     panics at the read()/write() CALL — so the backtrace points at the line supplying
     the address — with a message naming the port kind+index, the address, the size
     and the valid range.
     Rejected alternatives: X (what SV does on an out-of-range read) and wrap (what
     real address decoding does). Both silently substitute a value, turning an
     addressing bug into a wrong-answer bug downstream. When this was decided there
     was no transpiled Memory path to be faithful to, so no sim≡synth pressure and
     the diagnostic was worth more than the fidelity. THAT PREMISE EXPIRED on
     2026-08-24 — see the REVISIT item below.
     Pinned by tests/memory_multiport_arbitration.rs::out_of_range (4 tests, incl. the
     boundary address and "panics at the call, not at the edge").
  ✔ Memory TRANSPILES  [DONE 2026-08-24, user-scoped]
     Was: "Memory does not transpile AT ALL" — the largest single hole in the
     project's central claim. Now: single-cycle (READ_LAT = WRITE_LAT = 1)
     ReadFirst memories lower, and examples/memory/dual_port_ram.rs has
     sim ≡ transpiled SV under Verilator (tests/dual_port_ram_equivalence.rs) on
     top of its existing sim ≡ hand-written-SV check — so the generated SV is
     transitively anchored to third-party hardware. Full write-up in the
     TRANSPILATION section above (fix E).
  ✔ Pipelined latency: READ_LAT / WRITE_LAT > 1  [DONE 2026-08-24]
     The last Memory construct to reach the transpiled path. A read port becomes a
     register chain — stage READ_LAT-1 is the output, stage 0 holds what the most
     recent edge captured:
         mem_rd0_q0 <= mem_rd0_data;   // capture
         mem_rd0_q1 <= mem_rd0_q0;     // shift
     Written as ONE always_ff block, non-blocking assignment gives every RHS its
     pre-edge value — which IS the simulator's "shift toward the output, then
     capture into stage 0". A write port's stage 0 needs no register (filled by the
     write() call, consumed the same cycle), so WRITE_LAT yields WRITE_LAT-1 stage
     registers and the commit reads the last one.
     TWO EARLIER DECISIONS THAT LATENCY CHANGES, both silent-one-cycle-shift risks:
       1. WHICH NET A SAME-EDGE CONSUMER READS. A register update latched at the
          capture edge needs the value the output will hold AFTER that edge. At
          LAT=1 that is the live array read; deeper it is stage READ_LAT-2, read at
          its pre-edge value inside always_ff. ram_r3w1 (READ_LAT=3) exists so a
          chain deeper than two cannot be right by accident.
       2. WHICH WRITE THE WriteFirst MUX FORWARDS. It must be the write that
          COMMITS at this edge — at WRITE_LAT=2 the pipeline stage, NOT the freshly
          staged nets, which will not reach the array for another cycle.
     → tests/pipelined_memory_equivalence.rs (5 tests): READ_LAT=2/WRITE_LAT=2,
       READ_LAT=3/WRITE_LAT=1, READ_LAT=1/WRITE_LAT=2 WriteFirst, shape pins, and a
       check that the configurations are DISTINGUISHABLE — otherwise all three
       would pass against a transpiler that flattened every memory to one cycle.
       The reference model is written from the CONTRACT (latency as delay,
       read-during-write as which side of the commit a capture sees), not
       transcribed from copper-core/src/memory.rs, and matched on the first run.
     BOTH NEGATIVE CONTROLS MEASURED: making the same-edge form read stage LAT-1
     failed r2w2 and r3w1 while leaving r1w2_wf green; forwarding the freshly
     staged write instead of the committing stage failed only r1w2_wf. Each
     sabotage broke exactly the test built for it.
     NOTE the net names gained a stage index (`_q0`, `_v0`, `_wr0_s1_addr`), so the
     earlier shape pins were updated — and register_reconciliation's synthetic-name
     matcher now covers both pipeline shapes.
     THE CPUs STILL DO NOT TRANSPILE (re-measured): rv32i_cpu uses READ_LAT=2
     memories, but is blocked on the control_extract panic (cause G) and
     rv32i_cpu_pipelined on `Vec<Bits<32>>` ports (cause F). Memory is no longer
     what stops either.
  ✔ Multi-phase memory access  [DONE 2026-08-24]
     The address buses are combinational and PHASE-GATED, so outside its staging
     phase a read port's address reads '0 — a result observed in a later phase
     cannot be a continuous read of the array. The lowering now emits a read
     pipeline stage:
         always_ff @(posedge clk) begin
             rom_rd0_q <= rom_rd0_data;   // UNGUARDED, deliberately
             rom_rd0_v <= rom_rd0_en;
         end
     Unguarded is the faithful choice: advance_read_pipelines is a plain clock
     listener in copper-core/src/memory.rs — the memory knows nothing about phases,
     and its pipeline advances on every edge. Outside the staging phase the enable
     is 0, so _v goes false, which is exactly is_ready() after a cycle with no
     staged address. The captured word survives one cycle and no longer, in both.
     TWO FORMS, and picking wrong shifts a cycle EITHER WAY:
       - a consumer that latches at the SAME edge as the capture (a register update
         in the staging phase) reads the COMBINATIONAL value — the pipeline
         register has not updated yet at that edge;
       - everything else reads the REGISTER.
     Chosen per (phase, region) by vlir_lower::mem_binding.
     ✔ BUG FOUND AND FIXED, in code shipped earlier the same day: a single-tick
       loop whose post-tick segment drives an output straight from data() — no
       mediating register — read the continuous array net, so it tracked the
       address being staged for the NEXT edge. A full cycle early. It shipped
       because every memory test up to then put a register in between.
       Regression: tests/multiphase_memory_equivalence.rs::single_tick_direct_read.
     ☐ STILL REFUSED (pinned, with the counterexample kept in the fixture as
       `mp_rom`): a plain `Out` driven straight from a read result in a phase that
       does not stage it. Neither emitted form is right — a continuous assign
       tracks the pipeline into the phases that do not observe it, and the usual
       implicit-hold conversion latches at the end of the OBSERVING phase, one edge
       after the capture (measured: a full cycle late on every sampled value). The
       error points at RegOut, which works — the same Out/RegOut distinction the
       pre-tick alignment guardrail makes. Resolving it means reworking how
       split_output_regs interacts with expression timing; that is a semantics
       change to shared codegen machinery, not a memory detail.
     → tests/multiphase_memory_equivalence.rs (5 tests): the single-tick direct
       read, cross-phase into a register, cross-phase into a RegOut, shape pins for
       BOTH forms (so a future change cannot collapse them back into one — which is
       the bug above), and the rejection.
  ✔ WriteFirst read-during-write  [DONE 2026-08-24]
     ReadFirst needed no logic — the non-blocking write commits at the edge, so a
     continuous read of the array already sees the pre-write contents. WriteFirst
     cannot work that way (the array does not hold the new value until after the
     edge), so the read port's output net becomes a same-address forwarding mux:
         assign mem_rd0_data =
             ((mem_wr0_en && (mem_wr0_addr == mem_rd0_addr)) ? mem_wr0_data
                                                            : mem[mem_rd0_addr]);
     MUX ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING: with several write ports aimed at one address, the
     forwarded value must be the one the array ends up holding. The simulator
     commits writes in ascending port order, so the HIGHEST index wins — the rule
     tests/memory_multiport_arbitration.rs establishes at four ports, where "highest
     index", "second one" and "last issued" finally disagree. The mux therefore
     checks the highest index FIRST.
     → tests/write_first_memory_equivalence.rs (5 tests). Two things could be wrong
     and each has its own check:
       1. forwarding missing — a WriteFirst that silently behaved like ReadFirst
          would pass every WriteFirst-only check, since the modes agree on every
          cycle EXCEPT a same-address read/write. So both modes run on identical
          stimulus and must disagree on exactly those cycles.
       2. mux order wrong — ram_wf_priority drives two write ports at one address
          with different data.
     BOTH NEGATIVE CONTROLS MEASURED, not argued: forcing ReadFirst made the
     WriteFirst equivalence fail at cycles 2 and 5 (0xAA vs 0x55) while ReadFirst
     stayed green; reversing the mux order made only the priority test fail
     (0xB0 vs 0xB1). The single-port test correctly stayed green for the second —
     priority is unobservable with one write port.
     FALLOUT worth knowing: this surfaced a latent hole in copper-analysis.
     `reference_sv_registers` strips bracket groups, so `mem[a] <= d` reduced to
     `mem <= d` and a memory array was reported as a flip-flop named `mem`, which
     no source-level inference can produce — register_reconciliation failed on the
     new fixtures. Unpacked-array declarations are now excluded there (with two
     unit tests, including one that a bit-assigned register `q[3] <= …` is STILL a
     register). The same hole was latent for any hand-written reference SV with a
     RAM — examples/memory/sv/dual_port_ram.sv declares `reg [15:0] ram [1023:0]`.
  ✔ from_fn / from_contents preload equivalence through the transpiled path
     [DONE 2026-08-24, user chose the `initial` block over `$readmemh`]
     → tests/preloaded_memory_equivalence.rs — sim ≡ transpiled SV for BOTH
     constructors, on ROMs (1 read port, 0 write ports) so nothing but the preload
     can make the output right: a missing `initial` block reads as zeros instead of
     hiding behind a write. Addresses are driven out of fill order, so a reversed
     or off-by-one fill cannot pass either. Plus shape pins.
     THE KEY DESIGN POINT: the transpiler does NOT evaluate the preload — it emits
     the description. `from_fn(clk, N, |i| f(i))` becomes the fill LOOP:
         initial begin
             for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) rom[i] = 16'(((i * 32'd3) + 32'd7));
         end
     That is what makes `from_fn` representable at all without running Rust, and it
     is why the closure had to become a real FIR node (`ExprType::Closure`) rather
     than falling into the parser's raw-text catch-all. `vec![…]` likewise now
     desugars structurally to an Array/Repeat (`desugar_vec`, alongside the
     existing `desugar_matches`) instead of staying an opaque ExprMacro — the
     contents of a preload have to be visible to later phases.
     COST OF THE FORM, stated plainly: `initial` is what Verilator runs at time 0
     and what FPGA tools read to infer an initialized block RAM, but most ASIC
     flows ignore it. A preloaded memory is a simulation-and-FPGA construct by
     nature. That is a property of the construct, not of this lowering.
     WIDTH GOTCHA worth remembering: a fill body like `i * 3 + 7` has no inferable
     width, and an unresized assignment into the array is a Verilator width warning
     — fatal under `-Wall`. Preload words therefore get an explicit `width'(…)`
     cast whenever they are not already known to be element-width (stricter than
     `resize_to_target`, which leaves unknown widths alone).
     STILL REFUSED, each pinned in unsupported_constructs.rs:
       - contents computed at run time — `from_contents(clk, flat.clone())`, which
         is exactly what examples/cpu/rv32i_cpu.rs does. No source-level form to
         emit; a zero-filled array would be a silently wrong design.
       - a fill that is not an inline closure (a named fn / a bound closure)
       - a preload that reads one of the module's own signals. The simulator
         evaluates a captured port ONCE at construction; an `initial` block samples
         it at time 0. Two different things that look alike in source.
  ☐ Memory RULES — no longer unimplemented constructs, but shapes that must not
     lower silently. Each pinned with its own diagnostic in
     copper-codegen/tests/unsupported_constructs.rs, measured against
     `single_cycle_readfirst_memory_is_supported`, the baseline that DOES transpile:
       - two accesses to one port in a cycle (the sim keeps the last; one physical
         address bus cannot)
       - `data()` / `is_ready()` observed before the tick that produces them
       - a plain `Out` driven straight from a read result across phases (use RegOut)
       - contents computed at run time, a non-inline `from_fn` fill, or a preload
         that reads one of the module's own signals
       - READ_LAT / WRITE_LAT of 0
  ☐ REVISIT out-of-range addressing now that a synthesised counterpart EXISTS.
     The deliberate panic (copper-core/src/memory.rs::out_of_range) was decided on
     diagnostic grounds precisely because there was nothing to be faithful to.
     There is now: SystemVerilog reads X out of range, and the emitted address net
     is clog2(depth) bits, so a wider address truncates in the SV where the
     simulator panics. Not a divergence any passing test reaches (the sim panics
     first), but it is a real sim-vs-synth difference that is now decidable.

